{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1530314-how-to-storytell-hisil","id":1530314,"title":"How to storytell Hisil?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-02-08T14:16:31-08:00","id":1530314,"title":"How to storytell Hisil?","content":"Hello, I am quite new to Chronicles of Darkness. I am trying to create a story in which the mortal characters find a gate to Hisil, and are trying to get back. It is not a Werewolf game per se, but since Werewolves are probably closest to Hisil, I thought I would ask here.<br /> <br /> I understand that there are spirits of many things. Regular things (animals, trees, buildings, cars), emotions (fear, love), and then many more complex spirits, like hybrid spirits, or other creatures. I have read that spirits tend to feed on essence, either hunting other similar spirits, or obtaining the essence from the resonant things in the regular world. I also understand that they tend to form courts of spirits of similar kinds ,when they have sufficient rank.<br /> <br /> Now, my problem is to imagine all the possible things the characters would encounter in the city. There is many mundane things there could be spirits of. Tables, cups, cars, roads, buildings, lamps, plants, human emotions, electricity, candles, cats, concrete, glass, etc. Particularly the very lowest ranking spirits should be almost at every place where there are similarly resonant things in the Fallen world. How would you describe this complexity? What will the characters see once on the other side? Is is like the city in the normal world, just the objects there are (sleeping low ranking) spirits, so instead of furniture in Hisil apartments, one would find respective spirits in its place, being regularly collected by a sentient furniture spirit that goes house by house? Does basically everything consist of these low ranking spirits that are replacements of the mundane objects, and the intelligent denizens of the Hisil are more rare, and not so omnipresent? How would you describe these low ranking object-like spirits?<br /> <br /> How to construct the spirit courts, when there is so many possible courts? Do you have some favourite ones for the city? (Should there be literarly thousands of them, per category each?)<br /> <br /> Also, when reading the part from the Werewolf rules, I was surprised that the spirits are rather very stylized, and also very specific.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Blightlings (Iri Thim) are spirits of urban decay, feeding on the Essence of once-proud cities rotting. They resemble street people with broken, ruined bodies made of torn asphalt and garbage.<br /> <br /> Glass eyes (Anzah-Haz) are spirits of surveillance cameras, serving as lookouts and watchdogs for more powerful spirits. Each resembles a tall, thin human made of steel with a cyclopean glass lens-eye, looking down on passersby. Glass eyes are notorious gossips and snitches, always eager to offer up things they\u2019ve seen in exchange for Essence.<br /> <br /> Vermin (Nihhilim) are rat spirits, gathering in huge broods beneath the streets and behind alley fences. Many packs suspect that Nihhilim serve the Beshilu, but they\u2019re too cowardly for that. They prefer to avoid werewolves\u2019 anger, creeping into the aftermath of hunts to pick at the remains.<br /> <br /> Travels Below (Thifulmatha) are the spirits of subway systems, resembling skeletal, steel serpents with wide eyes. They are punctual to a fault, forcing anything that tries to delay them aside.<br /> <br /> Allholders (Gibsur) are the spirits of big-box stores and supermarkets, the corpulent landowners and slum-lords of the spirit courts. Unlike their smaller, shadier cousins such as the spirits of gas stations and quick-marts, the spirits of out-of-town mass retail pride themselves on fair dealing, and see themselves as genial hosts for lesser spirits of money, family, and the many goods that pass through their doors. Packs more often see them as parasites, extorting Essence from the hursahim too weak to escape their clutches.<br /> <br /> Darkenings (Uthsu) are some of the most common emotion spirits, drawing Essence from human grief. They congregate in hospitals, graveyards, funeral parlors, even war memorials. They appear as dark fog banks with churning black coils barely visible inside </div> </div> </div>I imagined always something very similar to the original (like spirit of a building really taking shape and size of the building, towering above the whole city, slowly moving through it). Are the spirits supposed by more methaphorical representations of the essence they consume? More humanoid-like, at least? <br /> <br /> Thank you in advance for the answers!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24286-irigi","name":"irigi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24286_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-08T14:43:32-08:00","id":1530317,"title":"","content":"First, yes, pretty much everything has a spirit. That said, I wouldn't get caught up on minutia, don't worry if there's a spirit of a coffee cup sitting on a desk or whatever. Rank 1spirits slumbering and doing nothing are scenery, not much more. This is the status of most spirits in the Hisil.<br /> <br /> Beyond that though, is where it gets interesting. Things will often resemble but not mirror, the physical counterparts. Look at the subway spirits as an example, they're probably about the same size, but they have an eerie and haunted life to them resembling skeletal snakes rather than just train cars. But they can still serve their function, you can still climb inside and ride, though at your own risk. That said, also don't be afraid to embellish, if you think of some spooky description for something, go with it. Werewolf is still a horror game after all.<br /> <br /> <br /> You mentioning the spirit of a building, they are going to be large spirits resembling a building most likely, but they're also a host to other spirits that dwell inside. Think of it like this. A spirit of of joy lives inside the building spirit. It feeds off the joy resonance of the children who visit Build a Bear. In turn it pays a tithe to the building spirit, who wouldn't otherwise be able to feed on that type of resonance. Then a store over maybe it's a fashion spirit. Maybe some animal spirits down at the pet store. Maybe some spirit(s) of protection or security tied to the security guards' office. You can view that as a court.<br /> <br /> But that spirit isn't the big fish, maybe it pays a tithe of essence up to a shopping center spirit. Maybe that shopping center spirit pays it's tithe up to a city spirit. It's just whatever you can imagine.<br /> <br /> That said, always remember, the Hisil is hungry. Spirits are immortal, but to sustain themselves they must feed. A hungry spirit is a desperate spirit and spirits can't just go down to the grocery store and buy a 12 pack of essence. It would just be so much easier if they were on this side of the gauntlet instead of being trapped a world away and feeding on what flows through the gauntlet, but there's these mean bully werewolves who don't want you to be sated and happy, they want you hungry, they want you to suffer. If only some of the good guy werewolves, the ashenga, would come along to help you out, they're nice.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27618,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","user":"ShadowcatX","date":". 02-08-2024, 05:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-08T19:21:59-08:00","id":1530335,"title":"","content":"The thing to know about powerful spirits is that most of them don't get to the Rank of Jaggling without transcending their origins as spirits of a single thing; spirits become less conceptually pure as they rise in Rank, and the conscious decision to join a descant corresponds with the capacity for a spirit to go from a self-concept of &quot;the spirit of This Dog&quot; to &quot;a spirit of Dogs.&quot;<br /> <br /> The thing to know about the urban Shadow is that places where the Gauntlet is thicker tend to be more abstracted from their material counterparts, like an artist's impression made through a foggy glass or an AI upscaling of a artifacted JPEG. Things move around in the same basic patterns, but a train engine is an Iron Horse and an assault rifle spits teeth, that sort of thing. At the basest level, things have the vague shape of their material basis and lack the detail to say they look much different, but motes and lesser Gafflings are low enough on the totem pole to be more scenery than individual actors in the Shadow's political stage.<br /> <br /> The thing to know about the Shadow in general is that it is specifically not a humanocentric reflection of the world; its denizens loosely resemble living creatures, but the spirit of a building is more likely to resemble a sessile being like coral than a giant moving through the city, unless the perceptions of the world around it and its own predations have shifted its Essence to be capable of doing so \u2014 maybe the shadow of a skyscraper sundialing its way across town in the eyes of children and some particularly unfortunate birds and bats could produce something out of a kaiju film, perhaps the corporate office has worked out how to send an agent, etc.<br /> <br /> The spirits who have a starting advantage in terms of decision-making are those born from emotions and sentient animals, while everything else \u2014 objects, plants, clouds \u2014 have to stick around long enough without dying to potentially boot themselves out of dormancy. Some spirits have an after-start advantage instead or as well \u2014 being born in an out-of-the-way location where compatible Essence still shows up helps, as does being conceptual poison to anything that's likely to be in your area or spawning outside the feeding range of any pesky omnivores \u2014 but the important thing that the most common active spirits demonstrate is that the Shadow runs on the logic of the animal kingdom with the intelligence ceiling and the weirdness cranked way up. This is what the game means when it describes the Shadow and its spirits as <i>alien</i>, because you're dealing with beings from a baseline of &quot;suppose we made Really Smart Cats&quot; or &quot;imagine if storm clouds hunted each other for sport.&quot;<br /> <br /> The other thing is that, after waking up and getting enough power offer some innate resistance to binding magic, spirits gain Rank when they gain <i>importance</i> in the world. That's where the politics come into play and why spirits who're still caught up in Being The Essence Of A Bed are functional non-entities until they get their act together. Spirit courts and broods and ilthum are semideliberate constructions based on the things that have power in a locale and the dynamics between them (magath can often occupy a high-Ranking dead-end niche in these structures for the simple reason of being dangerous pariahs that have to be tacitly acknowledged as symbols of something gone wrong).<br /> <br /> Is the nearby mountain important enough that people still talk about how to approach it and animals still live on it? It's probably ruling a spirit court by virtue of its power and impact on the surrounding spirits, even if it's physically indistinguishable from a big stone tortoise on the edge of town. Has the local big box superstore crushed the soul of its franchised municipality? The goods and services it sells will leave a mark on the Shadow while it buzzes and hums like a big rectangular antfarm-beehive. A massive biker gang controlling the steets heralds a wolfpack of motorcycle-spirits attended by fear and smoke made flesh; the pets and properties of the inner city pay tribute to them in sacrifice and servitude because of what they all are and how they relate to each other and the facts of power.<br /> <br /> Bringing it back around: most spirits aren't going to look human without particular quirks in their development (or a meatsuit); most spirits worth talking to will look like living beings (or at least animate constructs, for varying degrees of animation) because the ones not worth talking to largely look like basic shapes of the thing that they Are; regardless of how conscious they are, spirits can generally speak or at least understand the First Tongue and react to stimuli like creatures with the same proclivities (bird-motes are likely to go after seeds, cold-motes will react to heat, etc) \u2014 the Shadow speaks to itself, and much of it is dreaming. Those parts of it that are awake bring uncanny intellect and cunning (and supernatural powers of every stripe) to all the survival strategies biology and its relatives (memetics, etc) have produced across the eons, from the hive-mind problem-solving of an ant colony to the landscape-altering effects of wolves hunting deer to the wasp-summoning chemical reaction of certain plants. Some of the products of this system \u2014 most magath, for instance \u2014 are going to be weird and upsetting to look at because they're made of parts that don't quite go together (even if you can piece together a logic for them), and the higher up the chain you go, the more likely you'll find something with a name that doesn't fit in a human mouth and a shape that doesn't remotely match the physical reality of any of its components.<br /> <br /> tl;dr Design the Shadow of an area like you're making a metaphorical plant-and-animal ecosystem based around Significant Aspects of that region, remember that almost none of its members look or think anything like humans, remember that Gauntlet strength affects how closely it resembles the material world, and remember that most active spirits are based on things that can literally or metaphorically move under their own power and direction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-09T00:31:51-08:00","id":1530346,"title":"","content":"Thank you both for great answers, that helps a lot!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24286-irigi","name":"irigi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24286_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}